x86: emulate cooperative userspace TSC reads from the Harmony clock

CR4.TSD is the stock-KVM enforcement backstop for ring-3 RDTSC and RDTSCP:
KVM cannot be configured to exit those instructions on the hosted runners.
Patch 0003 already arms TSD for a Harmony exec image, but an unhandled #GP
turns every Go counter read into SIGSEGV. Decode only the faulting user
instruction, safely fetch its bytes, and emulate the two counter reads from
the materialized pvclock page. The guest counter is already in TSC ticks at
guest_clock_hz; do not convert the nanosecond field. RDTSCP receives the
ABI-pinned vCPU index (zero) as TSC_AUX.

Each emulated read rings the existing execution tick before sampling the page.
That gives counter polling loops a deterministic forward step while retaining
the same exit-count clock used by clock_gettime and the VMM's TSC oracle.
Malformed or unavailable pages, nonzero #GP error codes, unreadable code, and
every other instruction continue through Linux's normal #GP path and retain
PR_TSC_SIGSEGV behavior.

Outside a Harmony clock boot, PR_SET_TSC keeps normal Linux behavior. Inside
Harmony, TIF_NOTSC enforces virtual counter reads even if userspace explicitly
requests PR_TSC_SIGSEGV; it is not a separate signal-only mode.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a diff under the Harmony
repository's designated guest-kernel patch exception.

